  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the corridor, Ainura introduces her niece Gulnara on her very first workday after the girl accidentally bumps into Lyova. Meanwhile, Elena admires a handsome suit in the closet that Viktor has not worn in ten years.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Осторожнее!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ainura",
      "t": "Лёва, не злись.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ainura",
      "t": "Она моя племянница.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ainura",
      "t": "Сегодня первый день работает.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "ainura",
      "t": "Не знает ещё, как себя вести.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничего.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Собираешься?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Слушай, какой красивый костюм!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Чего ты его не носишь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, не знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Ну чего ты ходишь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Вот сядь и не напрягайся.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Осторожнее!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'More carefully!' The meaning hinges on 'more carefully' working together with 'more carefully' in spoken Russian. Ainura answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ainura",
      "t": "Лёва, не злись.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Lyova, do not be angry.' Notice the reflexive suffix on the verb meaning 'get angry', turning the action back on the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ainura",
      "t": "Она моя племянница.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She is my niece.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'she' and finishing on 'niece'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ainura",
      "t": "Сегодня первый день работает.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She is working her first day today.' Notice how Russian expresses 'tonight' and 'works' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "ainura",
      "t": "Не знает ещё, как себя вести.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She does not know how to behave yet.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'not' before landing on 'to lead'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничего.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is nothing.' Lyova brings out the contrast between 'nothing' and 'nothing' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Собираешься?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you getting ready?' Elena raises the pitch on 'getting ready' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Слушай, какой красивый костюм!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Listen, what a handsome suit!' Elena brings out the contrast between 'listen' and 'what' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Чего ты его не носишь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why do you not wear it?' In Russian, the idea of 'what' flows naturally into 'wear' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, не знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, I do not know.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'come on' and finishing on 'know'. Elena answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Ну чего ты ходишь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well why are you walking around?' Elena raises the pitch on 'are walking' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Вот сядь и не напрягайся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Sit down right here and do not strain yourself.' Elena brings out the contrast between 'here' and 'sit down' in a few short words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
